📘 Safe Handling of Computer Components – Quick Summary

1. Why Safe Handling Matters

  - Prevent ESD (Electrostatic Discharge) – can damage microvolt-level components
  - Avoid personal injury – sharp edges, electrical hazards
  - Ensure longevity and stability of components

2. ESD Protection

  - Method	Purpose
  - Anti-static wrist strap	Grounds you to prevent discharge
  - Anti-static mat	Dissipates static charge
  - Touch metal before handling	Pre-discharge built-up static
  - Anti-static bags	Store/transport components safely

3. Handling Specific Components

  - Component	How to Handle
  - Motherboard	Hold by edges; avoid circuitry/pins
  - CPU	Hold by sides; avoid pins/connectors
  - RAM	Insert firmly but don't force; ensure proper alignment
  - HDD/SSD	Avoid dropping; avoid pressure on sensitive parts

4. Power Supply Safety

  - Always unplug before working inside
  - Use a power supply tester before installation
  - Never handle connectors with wet/damp hands

5. Cable Management Tips

  - Keep cables organized for better airflow and cooling
  - Use cable ties or Velcro straps
  - Ensure proper grounding to prevent shorts

6. Workspace Best Practices

  - Work on clean, flat surface
  - Avoid heavy objects on delicate components
  - Ensure components are properly seated before powering on
  - Maintain optimal room temperature
  - Avoid excessive humidity (condensation/corrosion)
  - Keep food and drinks away from workstation

7. Common Mistakes

  - Forgetting to ground yourself
  - Handling components too roughly
  - Forcing parts into place (damages pins/connectors)
  - Ignoring cooling (e.g., running with a broken fan)
